:minmax(0,1fr)}}@media print{article[data-g9-role="CORE2A"] .g9-split{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,68fr) minmax(0,32fr);gap:12px}}@media (min-width:1100px){article[data-g9-role="CORE2B"] .g9-split{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,68fr) minmax(0,32fr);gap:22px;align-items:start}article[data-g9-role="CORE2B"] .g9-split-primary-only,article[data-g9-role="CORE2B"] .g9-split-support-only{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr)}}@media print{article[data-g9-role="CORE2B"] .g9-split{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,68fr) minmax(0,32fr);gap:12px}}</style></head><body data-core="CORE1A" data-blueprint-ref="BP-CORE1A-CONSTRUCTION@1.7.0"><header class="g9-shell-header" data-g9-shell-header><div class="g9-header-inner"><a href="../../../index.html" class="g9-brand"><span class="logo-icon">⚡</span><span class="brand-title">Grade9V3.5</span><span class="g9-brand-badge">Learner Platform</span></a><nav class="g9-header-nav" aria-label="Portal Navigation"><a data-g9-home href="../../../index.html">Home</a><a href="physics/index.html">Physics</a><a href="chemistry/index.html">Chemistry</a><a href="mathematics/index.html">Mathematics</a><a href="../../../question-bank/index.html">Question Bank</a></nav><div class="g9-header-actions"><button type="button" class="g9-header-btn g9-search-btn" data-g9-action="search" title="Search Grade9V3 (Ctrl/⌘ K)" aria-label="Search"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🔍</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Search</span></button><button type="button" class="g9-header-btn g9-display-btn" data-g9-action="display" title="Display & Theme Settings" aria-label="Display & Theme"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🌙 / ☀️</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Display</span></button><a class="g9-header-btn g9-pdf-link" data-g9-action="pdf" data-g9-pdf-link aria-label="Open the PDF of this page to print it" href="core1a.pdf" title="Open the PDF of this page to print it">PDF</a></div></div><div data-g9-search-panel hidden><input data-g9-search-input type="search" aria-label="Search this page"></div><div data-g9-display-panel hidden><button type="button" data-g9-font="dec">A-</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="reset">A</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="inc">A+</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="light">Light</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="dark">Dark</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="dec">Zoom -</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="reset">100%</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="inc">Zoom +</button></div></header><div class="g9-concept-triad-bar"><div class="g9-triad-inner"><div class="g9-triad-context"><a href="physics/index.html">Physics</a> / <a href="topics/nlm/index.html">Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</a> / <span aria-current="page">Learn</span></div><span class="g9-triad-concept-title">Concept: Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</span><div class="g9-triad-actions"><a class="g9-triad-btn" href="core1.html">Orientation</a><a class="g9-triad-btn active" href="core1a.html">📖 Learn</a><a class="g9-triad-btn" href="core1b.html">Recall</a><a class="g9-triad-btn" href="core2.html">✍️ Practice</a><a class="g9-triad-btn" href="core2a.html">Apply</a><a class="g9-triad-btn" href="core2b.html">Transfer</a><a class="g9-triad-btn g9-btn-qb" href="../../../question-bank/index.html">All questions in QB &rarr;</a></div></div></div><noscript>Answers open after you attempt; this page needs JavaScript.</noscript><main><h1>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model: Construction</h1><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-contents"><summary>Contents and concept route</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><section class="g9-core1a-book" data-g9-bucket-orientation data-g9-bucket-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION"><p class="g9-prov">Concept Book</p><h2>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</h2><div class="g9-bucket-orientation-grid"><div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_promise"><h4>Learning promise</h4><p>Independent perpendicular component motion in one frame, constant-acceleration component equations with common time, and the ideal near-Earth projectile specialization for horizontal and oblique launch questions.</p></div><nav class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_route" data-g9-concept-route aria-label="Concept Book route"><h4>Concept route</h4><ol><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS"><a href="#MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a><ol><li><a href="#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1">Solve each axis on its own, but read both coordinates at one shared instant before calling the pair a position.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION"><a href="#MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</a><ol><li><a href="#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1">Check on each axis that its acceleration component is constant, then run the one-dimensional equations once per axis with one shared time.</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL"><a href="#MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a><ol><li><a href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1">Decide from what acts after release whether the gravity-only model applies, and only then put a_x = 0 and a_y = −g into the component equations.</a></li><li><a href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2">Name the requested event as a component condition before solving for its time: v_y = 0 at the apex, Δy = 0 at a return to launch height.</a></li><li><a href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3">Write the landing event from the actual drop while keeping the actual u_y, then read every requested quantity at that one event time.</a></li></ol></li></ol></nav></div><aside><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="foundation_route"><h4>Foundation route</h4><ul><li data-g9-foundation-ref="BUCKET-PHY-VEC-ADD-SUB" data-g9-availability="unlinked">Vector addition, subtraction and orientation <span class="g9-availability-note">— direct route unavailable in this product</span></li><li data-g9-foundation-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-1D-MOTION" data-g9-availability="unlinked">One-dimensional motion <span class="g9-availability-note">— direct route unavailable in this product</span></li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_contract"><h4>Model contract</h4><ul><li>Use one declared non-rotating x-y frame for the whole event; both component equations use the same elapsed time.</li><li>When +y is chosen upward, the ideal projectile model uses a_x=0 and a_y=-g after release.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope_boundary"><h4>Outside this book</h4><ul><li>Calculus-based differentiation of a time-dependent position vector.</li><li>Derivation or memorization of a large catalogue of range/height/time-of-flight formulas as separate capabilities.</li><li>Trajectory-equation derivation as a first-slice requirement.</li><li>Air drag, Coriolis effects, variable gravity, target-on-incline projectile geometry and other advanced variants.</li></ul></div></aside></div></section></div></details><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock Medium concept Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Solve each axis on its own, but read both coordinates at one shared instant before calling the pair a position.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 1 of 5</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-next-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1" aria-label="Next section: Check on each axis that its acceleration component is constant, then run the one-dimensional equations once per axis with one shared time.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Conditions for this construction</h4><ul><li>Can read signed vector components in a declared x-y frame.</li></ul></div><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>Prerequisite reference</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-VECTOR-SIGNED-COMPONENT" data-bridged="true">Choosing axes and keeping components signed (Physics)</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D1-1"><strong>Choose one fixed x-y frame, one origin and one time origin for the whole plane-motion event.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A component value is meaningful only after its frame and sign convention are fixed.<br><em>Result:</em> one declared frame, origin, axis orientation and t=0</li><li data-g9-step="K2D1-2"><strong>Represent position, velocity and acceleration as signed x and y components in that same frame.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A plane vector is completely represented by its perpendicular Cartesian components once the frame is declared.<br><em>Result:</em> For x = 3t and y = 5t − t² at t = 1 s: (x, y) = (3, 4) m, (v_x, v_y) = (3, 3) m/s, (a_x, a_y) = (0, −2) m/s²</li><li data-g9-step="K2D1-3"><strong>Keep x-quantities in the x description and y-quantities in the y description while using one shared elapsed time.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The perpendicular component equations are independent in Cartesian kinematics, but both describe the same object during the same physical interval.<br><em>Result:</em> x(1) = 3 × 1 = 3 m uses only x-quantities; y(1) = 5 × 1 − 1² = 4 m uses only y-quantities; both use t = 1 s</li><li data-g9-step="K2D1-4"><strong>Recombine only component values that refer to the same frame and the same instant.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Mixing x at one time with y at another does not describe any single physical state of the object.<br><em>Result:</em> one physically valid plane state reconstructed from simultaneous components</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1-REP-KIN-2D-DRONE-SHARED-CLOCK" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-KIN-2D-DRONE-SHARED-CLOCK" data-g9-kind="VECTOR" data-g9-min-figure-text="14" data-reveal-stages="4" data-g9-stages-total="4" data-g9-stages="VIS-KIN-2D-DRONE-V0 VIS-KIN-2D-DRONE-V1 VIS-KIN-2D-DRONE-V2 VIS-KIN-2D-DRONE-V3" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><div class="g9-diagram-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Diagram; scroll horizontally when enlarged"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 720 510" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1-REP-KIN-2D-DRONE-SHARED-CLOCK-1--rep-kin-drone-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1-REP-KIN-2D-DRONE-SHARED-CLOCK-1--rep-kin-drone-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1-REP-KIN-2D-DRONE-SHARED-CLOCK-1--rep-kin-drone-title">Drone x(t) = 6t, y(t) = 8t − t²: both coordinates read at one shared instant</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1-REP-KIN-2D-DRONE-SHARED-CLOCK-1--rep-kin-drone-desc">Stage 1: a drone lifts off from its launch pad and flies along a curved path. Stage 2: one declared frame with origin O at the pad, +x east, +y up, and one shared clock with t = 0 at launch; the axes are marked at x = 12 and 24 m and y = 12 and 16 m. Stage 3: the two component descriptions kept apart: the x-description x(t) = 6t gives x(2) = 12 m and x(4) = 24 m; the y-description y(t) = 8t − t² gives y(2) = 12 m and y(4) = 16 m. Stage 4: reading both at t = 2 s gives the position (12, 12) m, marked on the path; at t = 4 s the drone is at (24, 16) m, also on the path; the mixed pair x(2 s), y(4 s) = (12, 16) m is marked off the path and rejected, because at x = 12 m the drone is at y = 12 m, so it never occupies (12, 16) m.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1-REP-KIN-2D-DRONE-SHARED-CLOCK-1--rep-kin-drone-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1-REP-KIN-2D-DRONE-SHARED-CLOCK-1--rep-kin-drone-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-DRONE-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A drone flies from its launch pad</text>\n    <line x1="30" y1="300" x2="470" y2="300" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <rect x="62" y="300" width="36" height="8" fill="currentColor"/>\n    <text x="104" y="322">launch pad</text>\n    <path d="M80 300 Q260 -84 440 300" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>\n    <g transform="translate(80 290)"><rect x="-12" y="-4" width="24" height="8" rx="2" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="-20" y1="-8" x2="-6" y2="-8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="6" y1="-8" x2="20" y2="-8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/></g>\n    <text x="100" y="278">drone</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-DRONE-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="80" y1="300" x2="470" y2="300" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1-REP-KIN-2D-DRONE-SHARED-CLOCK-1--rep-kin-drone-arrow)"/>\n    <line x1="80" y1="300" x2="80" y2="60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1-REP-KIN-2D-DRONE-SHARED-CLOCK-1--rep-kin-drone-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="476" y="305">+x (east), m</text>\n    <text x="88" y="60">+y (up), m</text>\n    <text x="58" y="320" text-anchor="middle">O</text>\n    <line x1="170" y1="295" x2="170" y2="305" stroke="currentColor"/>\n    <text x="170" y="344" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">12</text>\n    <line x1="260" y1="295" x2="260" y2="305" stroke="currentColor"/>\n    <text x="260" y="344" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">24</text>\n    <line x1="75" y1="156" x2="85" y2="156" stroke="currentColor"/>\n    <text x="70" y="161" text-anchor="end" font-size="14">12</text>\n    <line x1="75" y1="108" x2="85" y2="108" stroke="currentColor"/>\n    <text x="70" y="113" text-anchor="end" font-size="14">16</text>\n    <circle cx="514" cy="70" r="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M514 70 V60 M514 70 H522" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="492" y="104" font-weight="600">one shared clock t</text>\n    <text x="492" y="126">t = 0 at launch</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-DRONE-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="372" font-weight="600">x-description (x only)</text>\n    <text x="20" y="394">x(t) = 6t</text>\n    <text x="20" y="416">x(2) = 6 × 2 = 12 m</text>\n    <text x="20" y="438">x(4) = 6 × 4 = 24 m</text>\n    <text x="300" y="372" font-weight="600">y-description (y only)</text>\n    <text x="300" y="394">y(t) = 8t − t²</text>\n    <text x="300" y="416">y(2) = 8 × 2 − 2² = 12 m</text>\n    <text x="300" y="438">y(4) = 8 × 4 − 4² = 16 m</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-DRONE-V3" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="170" y1="300" x2="170" y2="88" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 4"/>\n    <circle cx="170" cy="156" r="7" fill="#1f6fb2"/>\n    <text x="182" y="182" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">t = 2 s: (12, 12) m ✓</text>\n    <circle cx="260" cy="108" r="6" fill="currentColor"/>\n    <text x="260" y="94" text-anchor="middle">t = 4 s: (24, 16) m</text>\n    <circle cx="170" cy="108" r="7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="184" y="70">x(2 s), y(4 s) = (12, 16) m ✗</text>\n    <text x="20" y="470">(12, 16) m mixes two instants: at x = 12 m the drone is at y = 12 m,</text>\n    <text x="20" y="492">so it never occupies (12, 16) m.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div><div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the drone&#x27;s flight from the pad without the component description.">1: Problem only</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Declare the origin, axes and one clock for the whole flight.">2: Frame and clock</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Evaluate x and y separately at 2 s and 4 s, each from its own function.">3: Component descriptions</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="3" data-g9-stage-desc="Pair only same-time values into positions and reject the mixed pair.">4: Same-time pairing</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the drone of x(t) = 6t, y(t) = 8t − t² in one frame with one clock, so each axis is evaluated on its own and only same-time values are paired into a position.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>Perpendicular components may be solved separately because each axis equation contains only that axis&#x27;s quantities, but they still describe one object at one physical time.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-KIN-2D-DISPLACEMENT"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>Each perpendicular displacement component follows the 1D constant-acceleration displacement relation over one common interval.</p></td><td><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Worked explanation</h4><p>A drone flies in a frame with origin at its launch pad, +x east, +y up and t = 0 at launch. Its position is x(t) = 6t m and y(t) = (8t − t²) m. Find its position at t = 2 s, and decide whether the pair x(2 s), y(4 s) is a position the drone ever occupies.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Follow the operation, its justification and its result.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>The frame, origin and clock are declared once for the whole flight, so both functions share one t.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>At t = 2 s: x(2) = 6 × 2 = 12 m and y(2) = 8 × 2 − 2² = 12 m, so the drone is at (12, 12) m.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>y(4) = 8 × 4 − 4² = 16 m, so the pair x(2 s), y(4 s) is (12, 16) m.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>x = 12 m happens only at t = 2 s, when y is 12 m rather than 16 m, so (12, 16) m is never a position of the drone.</p></div></li></ol><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>At t = 2 s the drone is at (12, 12) m. The pair (12, 16) m mixes two instants and is not a position the drone ever occupies.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>At t = 4 s the drone is really at (24, 16) m: substituting t = 4 into both functions gives a genuine position, and its x differs from the mixed pair&#x27;s.</p></div></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><h3>Solve each axis on its own, but read both coordinates at one shared instant before calling the pair a position.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Independent x and y motions may use different elapsed times.</li><li>The x-motion and y-motion are two different objects that can be paired arbitrarily later.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>At one instant 2 s after launch, may the x equation use t=2 s while the y equation uses another time?</li><li>May x(2 s) be combined with y(3 s) to describe the particle&#x27;s position?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Independence separates the axis equations; it does not give the same object two clocks.</li><li>No. A plane state is a simultaneous pair. Recombine x(t) and y(t) only at the same t in the same frame.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Substitute the reported t back into both component functions: each must return its reported coordinate. If one coordinate only matches at a different t, the pair is not a position.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Reverse the declared +y direction: every y-quantity must change sign together while no x-quantity changes, and the physical position stays the same.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A particle has x-velocity 6 m/s and y-velocity -8 m/s at t=3 s. Explain what &#x27;independent components&#x27; means and what must remain shared between the x and y descriptions.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>The x and y component equations are solved separately in the same frame, but both describe the same particle at the same t=3 s.</p><ol><li>Keep x quantities in the x equation and y quantities in the y equation.</li><li>Use one common elapsed time for the same physical instant.</li><li>Recombine components only after each axis is handled consistently.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2018 · Paper 2 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09">JEE (Advanced) · 2019 · Paper 2 · Q9</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q5</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q6</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09">JEE (Advanced) · 2024 · Paper 2 · Q9</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05">IIT-JEE · 2012 · Paper I · Q5</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2022 · Paper 1 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01">JEE (Advanced) · 2023 · Paper 1 · Q1</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15">JEE (Advanced) · 2025 · Paper 2 · Q15</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q26</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q11" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q11">JEE (Advanced) · 2014 · Paper 1 · Q11</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2026-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2026-P1-Q06">JEE (Advanced) · 2026 · Paper 1 · Q6</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2013-P1-Q07" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2013-P1-Q07">JEE (Advanced) · 2013 · Paper 1 · Q7</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-04APR-S2-Q29" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-04APR-S2-Q29">JEE Main · 2026 · Session 2 · 04 Apr 2026 · Shift 2 · Q29</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-visual-card" data-g9-component="INTERACTIVE_BRIDGE" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="VISUAL_CARD"><aside class="g9-interactive-bridge" data-g9-interactive-bridge><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-interactive">Try Visually</span><h3 class="g9-interactive-title">Motion in a Plane Research Suite</h3><p class="g9-interactive-desc">Explore this concept with interactive controls and real-time visual response.</p><a class="g9-bridge-link g9-action-interactive" data-g9-interactive-link href="physics/motion-2d/explorers/motion-in-a-plane/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try it visually &rarr;</a></aside><aside class="g9-interactive-bridge" data-g9-interactive-bridge><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-interactive">Try Visually</span><h3 class="g9-interactive-title">2D Motion Master Suite</h3><p class="g9-interactive-desc">Explore this concept with interactive controls and real-time visual response.</p><a class="g9-bridge-link g9-action-interactive" data-g9-interactive-link href="physics/motion-in-2d/explorers/motions_in_2d/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try it visually &rarr;</a></aside></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></article><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component Medium concept Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Check on each axis that its acceleration component is constant, then run the one-dimensional equations once per axis with one shared time.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 2 of 5</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1" aria-label="Previous section: Solve each axis on its own, but read both coordinates at one shared instant before calling the pair a position.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1" aria-label="Next section: Decide from what acts after release whether the gravity-only model applies, and only then put a_x = 0 and a_y = −g into the component equations.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Conditions for this construction</h4><ul><li>Can use the existing one-dimensional constant-acceleration model.</li><li>Can keep x/y components separate with one common time.</li></ul></div><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>Prerequisite reference</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-bridged="true"><a href="core1a.html#MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Represent one plane-motion event as independent signed x/y component histories that share one elapsed time.</a></li><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-KIN-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-bridged="true">Derive the constant-acceleration equations from one velocity-time graph (Physics)</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D2-1"><strong>Check separately whether a_x and a_y are constant over the interval before choosing constant-acceleration equations on either axis.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The familiar one-dimensional kinematic relations are exact only on an axis whose acceleration component is constant over the interval.<br><em>Result:</em> per-axis model-validity decision</li><li data-g9-step="K2D2-2"><strong>Apply the one-dimensional constant-acceleration relations independently to x and y using only that axis&#x27;s signed values and the same t.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Each Cartesian component obeys the same one-dimensional relation when its acceleration component is constant.<br><em>Result:</em> v_x=u_x+a_x t; v_y=u_y+a_y t; Delta x=u_x t+0.5a_x t^2; Delta y=u_y t+0.5a_y t^2</li><li data-g9-step="K2D2-3"><strong>If one axis determines an event time, reuse that same time on the other axis before recombining and checking the plane state.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The event occurs once for the object; a time obtained from one component is the clock value for every component at that same event.<br><em>Result:</em> event time transferred across axes, then one checked plane state</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK" data-g9-kind="VECTOR" data-g9-min-figure-text="14" data-reveal-stages="4" data-g9-stages-total="4" data-g9-stages="VIS-KIN-2D-PUCK-V0 VIS-KIN-2D-PUCK-V1 VIS-KIN-2D-PUCK-V2 VIS-KIN-2D-PUCK-V3" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><div class="g9-diagram-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Diagram; scroll horizontally when enlarged"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 760 390" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-title">Puck with u = (4 i + 6 j) m/s and a = (0 i − 2 j) m/s²: check each axis, then one shared time</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-desc">Stage 1: a puck on a tilted air table starts at the origin with velocity u = (4 i + 6 j) m/s and constant acceleration a = (0 i − 2 j) m/s², and follows a curved path back to its starting line. Stage 2: the table\'s frame, +x and +y, and a per-axis check: a_x = 0 is constant and a_y = −2 m/s² is constant, so the constant-acceleration equations are exact on x and on y. Stage 3: the equations run once per axis with one shared t: Δx = 4t and v_x = 4 on x; Δy = 6t − t² and v_y = 6 − 2t on y; the puck is next at y = 0 when t(6 − t) = 0, at the later root t = 6 s. Stage 4: the same t = 6 s is used on both axes: the puck is at x = 24 m, marked on the starting line, moving with v = (4 i − 6 j) m/s, drawn as an arrow. The drawing\'s vertical scale is stretched relative to its horizontal scale.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-PUCK-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A puck on a tilted air table</text>\n    <rect x="30" y="50" width="400" height="300" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <path d="M80 290 Q212 -34 344 290" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>\n    <circle cx="80" cy="290" r="8" fill="currentColor"/>\n    <line x1="80" y1="290" x2="119.6" y2="192.8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="112" y="262">u = (4 i + 6 j) m/s</text>\n    <line x1="405" y1="64" x2="405" y2="114" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="392" y="80" text-anchor="end">a = (0 i − 2 j) m/s²</text>\n    <text x="392" y="100" text-anchor="end">throughout</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-PUCK-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="80" y1="290" x2="420" y2="290" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-arrow)"/>\n    <line x1="80" y1="290" x2="80" y2="64" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="410" y="312">+x</text>\n    <text x="88" y="74">+y</text>\n    <text x="64" y="310">O</text>\n    <text x="460" y="60" font-weight="600">Check each axis first</text>\n    <text x="460" y="84">x: a_x = 0, constant ✓</text>\n    <text x="460" y="106">y: a_y = −2 m/s², constant ✓</text>\n    <text x="460" y="128">→ constant-a equations</text>\n    <text x="460" y="148">exact on x and on y</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-PUCK-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="460" y="186" font-weight="600">One shared t on both axes</text>\n    <text x="460" y="208">x: Δx = 4t,  v_x = 4</text>\n    <text x="460" y="230">y: Δy = 6t − t²,  v_y = 6 − 2t</text>\n    <text x="460" y="252">next y = 0: t(6 − t) = 0</text>\n    <text x="460" y="274" font-weight="600">later root t = 6 s</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-PUCK-V3" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <circle cx="344" cy="290" r="7" fill="#1f6fb2"/>\n    <line x1="344" y1="290" x2="366" y2="344" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1-REP-KIN-2D-PUCK-AXIS-CHECK-1--rep-kin-puck-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="318" y="280" text-anchor="end" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">t = 6 s: x = 24 m</text>\n    <text x="460" y="316" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">same t = 6 s on x and y:</text>\n    <text x="460" y="338">Δx = 4 × 6 = 24 m</text>\n    <text x="460" y="360">v = (4 i − 6 j) m/s</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div><div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the puck with its stated initial velocity and constant acceleration.">1: The puck&#x27;s launch</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Declare the frame and check that a_x and a_y are each constant.">2: Per-axis check</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Write each axis&#x27;s equations and solve the event on y.">3: One time on both axes</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="3" data-g9-stage-desc="Read x and the velocity at the same t = 6 s.">4: State at the event</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the air-table puck with u = (4, 6) m/s and a = (0, −2) m/s²: check that each axis&#x27;s acceleration is constant, run the one-dimensional equations once per axis, and carry the one event time t = 6 s across both axes.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>The one-dimensional constant-acceleration equations can be applied to each perpendicular component independently when a_x and a_y are each constant over the same interval.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-KIN-2D-VELOCITY"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>Each perpendicular velocity component follows the 1D constant-acceleration relation over one common interval.</p></td><td><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></td></tr><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-KIN-2D-DISPLACEMENT"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>Each perpendicular displacement component follows the 1D constant-acceleration displacement relation over one common interval.</p></td><td><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Worked explanation</h4><p>A puck on a tilted air table starts at the origin with velocity (4 i + 6 j) m/s, and its acceleration is (0 i − 2 j) m/s² throughout. Using the table&#x27;s x-y frame, decide which constant-acceleration equations apply on each axis, find when the puck is next at y = 0, and find its x-position and velocity at that instant.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Follow the operation, its justification and its result.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>a_x = 0 and a_y = −2 m/s² are both constant, so the constant-acceleration relations are exact on both axes.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>On y: Δy = 6t − 0.5 × 2 × t² = 6t − t². Setting Δy = 0 gives t(6 − t) = 0, and the later root is t = 6 s.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>On x, at the same t = 6 s: Δx = 4 × 6 = 24 m.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Velocity at t = 6 s: v_x = 4 m/s and v_y = 6 − 2 × 6 = −6 m/s, so v = (4 i − 6 j) m/s.</p></div></li></ol><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>The puck is next at y = 0 at t = 6 s, at x = 24 m, moving with v = (4 i − 6 j) m/s.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>v_y comes back with its starting size, 6 m/s, and the opposite sign, as v_y² = u_y² + 2a_y Δy requires when Δy = 0; v_x is unchanged because a_x = 0.</p></div></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><h3>Check on each axis that its acceleration component is constant, then run the one-dimensional equations once per axis with one shared time.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>The magnitude of acceleration should be inserted unchanged into both x and y equations.</li><li>If one acceleration component is constant, the constant-acceleration equations are automatically valid on both axes.</li><li>A time found from the x equation may be replaced by a different convenient time in the y equation.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If a=(10 i + 4 j) m/s², should both component equations use a=√116?</li><li>If a_x is constant but a_y changes with time, may the same constant-acceleration displacement formula be used exactly on y over the whole interval?</li><li>If x-motion says an event occurs at t=3 s, what time belongs in the y equation for that same event?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Use a_x=10 only in the x equation and a_y=4 only in the y equation; the magnitude is not either component.</li><li>No. Model validity is checked per axis. A constant a_x does not make a_y constant.</li><li>Use t=3 s. Both components describe the same event and therefore share one clock.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Every term of Δy = u_y t + 0.5 a_y t² must come out in metres: (m/s)(s) and (m/s²)(s²). A velocity term in m/s beside them means a t is missing.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Set a_x = 0: the x equations must reduce to uniform motion, v_x = u_x and Δx = u_x t, whatever happens on y.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A particle starts with u=(0,5) m/s and constant a=(10,4) m/s². Set up the x and y position equations at common time t without solving a particular numerical t.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>Use Delta x=0*t+0.5(10)t² and Delta y=5t+0.5(4)t² with the same t.</p><ol><li>Read signed component initial velocities and accelerations.</li><li>Apply the 1D constant-acceleration displacement relation separately.</li><li>Keep one common elapsed time.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15">JEE (Advanced) · 2025 · Paper 2 · Q15</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></article><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="SECTION_ROUTE" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-section-route"><summary>Sections</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><nav data-g9-section-route aria-label="Sections in this concept"><ol><li><a href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1">Decide from what acts after release whether the gravity-only model applies, and only then put a_x = 0 and a_y = −g into the component equations.</a></li><li><a href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2">Name the requested event as a component condition before solving for its time: v_y = 0 at the apex, Δy = 0 at a return to launch height.</a></li><li><a href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3">Write the landing event from the actual drop while keeping the actual u_y, then read every requested quantity at that one event time.</a></li></ol></nav></div></details></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Decide from what acts after release whether the gravity-only model applies, and only then put a_x = 0 and a_y = −g into the component equations.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 3 of 5</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1" aria-label="Previous section: Check on each axis that its acceleration component is constant, then run the one-dimensional equations once per axis with one shared time.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2" aria-label="Next section: Name the requested event as a component condition before solving for its time: v_y = 0 at the apex, Δy = 0 at a return to launch height.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Conditions for this construction</h4><ul><li>Can solve 2D constant-acceleration component equations.</li><li>If an oblique launch angle is given, angle-based vector decomposition is available as a separate prerequisite for that question.</li></ul></div><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>Prerequisite reference</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-bridged="true"><a href="core1a.html#MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">Apply constant-acceleration kinematics independently to x and y components of one plane-motion event.</a></li></ul></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D3-1"><strong>Decide whether the object is in ideal near-Earth free flight after release; neglect air resistance, declare +y, then set a_x=0 and a_y=-g.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The standard projectile equations are a conditional specialization of 2D constant acceleration, not an automatic label for every launched object.<br><em>Result:</em> gravity-only projectile acceleration state</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-2"><strong>Use supplied or separately resolved u_x and u_y with one common t in the horizontal and vertical component equations.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Horizontal, oblique and downward/upward initial components change the initial state, not the gravity-only model.<br><em>Result:</em> v_x=u_x; v_y=u_y-g t; Delta x=u_x t; Delta y=u_y t-0.5g t^2</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6" data-g9-kind="GRAPH" data-g9-min-figure-text="14" data-reveal-stages="4" data-g9-stages-total="4" data-g9-stages="VIS-KIN-2D-BALCONY-V0 VIS-KIN-2D-BALCONY-V1 VIS-KIN-2D-BALCONY-V2 VIS-KIN-2D-BALCONY-V3" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><div class="g9-diagram-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Diagram; scroll horizontally when enlarged"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 380" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6-1--rep-kin-balcony-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6-1--rep-kin-balcony-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6-1--rep-kin-balcony-title">A ball thrown from a balcony with u_x = 8 m/s and u_y = 6 m/s</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6-1--rep-kin-balcony-desc">Stage 1: a ball is thrown from a balcony with u_x = 8 m/s and u_y = 6 m/s; +y is upward and g = 10 m/s². Stage 2: after release only gravity acts and air resistance is negligible, so the ideal model applies with a = (0, −10) m/s². Stage 3: the component relations are v_x = 8, v_y = 6 − 10t, Δx = 8t and Δy = 6t − 5t². Stage 4: at t = 1 s the displacement is (8 i + 1 j) m and the velocity is (8 i − 4 j) m/s.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6-1--rep-kin-balcony-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6-1--rep-kin-balcony-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-BALCONY-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">A ball thrown from a balcony</text>\n    <rect x="30" y="200" width="100" height="170" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="100" y1="200" x2="140" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"/>\n    <circle cx="140" cy="200" r="7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="147" y1="200" x2="207" y2="200" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6-1--rep-kin-balcony-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="212" y="205">u_x = 8 m/s</text>\n    <line x1="140" y1="193" x2="140" y2="133" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6-1--rep-kin-balcony-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="148" y="140">u_y = 6 m/s</text>\n    <text x="470" y="60">+y upward; g = 10 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="470" y="82">Find the displacement and velocity</text>\n    <text x="470" y="104">1 s after release.</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-BALCONY-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="140" y1="215" x2="140" y2="265" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6-1--rep-kin-balcony-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="148" y="262">gravity only</text>\n    <text x="470" y="140" font-weight="600">Model gate</text>\n    <text x="470" y="162">after release only gravity acts;</text>\n    <text x="470" y="184">air resistance negligible:</text>\n    <text x="470" y="206">a = (0, −10) m/s²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-BALCONY-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="470" y="240" font-weight="600">Component relations</text>\n    <text x="470" y="262">v_x = 8, v_y = 6 − 10t</text>\n    <text x="470" y="284">Δx = 8t, Δy = 6t − 5t²</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-BALCONY-V3" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <path d="M140 200 L164 183.5 L188 170 L212 159.5 L236 152 L260 147.5 L284 146 L308 147.5 L332 152 L356 159.5 L380 170" fill="none" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>\n    <circle cx="380" cy="170" r="7" fill="#1f6fb2" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="380" y1="170" x2="440" y2="200" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1-REP-KIN-2D-BALCONY-8-6-1--rep-kin-balcony-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="160" y="320" fill="#1f6fb2">at t = 1 s: Δr = (8 i + 1 j) m</text>\n    <text x="160" y="342" fill="#1f6fb2">v = (8 i − 4 j) m/s</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div><div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the release point and the launch components.">1: The throw</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Decide the model from the post-release interactions.">2: Model gate</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Write the component relations on one clock.">3: Component relations</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="3" data-g9-stage-desc="Draw the path from the balcony for 1 s and read Δr and v.">4: At t = 1 s</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the ball thrown from the balcony with u_x = 8 m/s and u_y = 6 m/s, with the model decided from what acts after release.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>After release in the ideal near-Earth model, projectile motion is not a new set of laws: it is the same component kinematics with a_x=0 and a_y=-g (for +y upward).</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-PROJECTILE-COMPONENT-MODEL"><td><div class="g9-math" data-g9-math="mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></div></td><td><p>With +y upward, air resistance neglected and near-Earth constant g, projectile motion is 2D constant acceleration specialized to zero horizontal and downward vertical acceleration.</p></td><td><ul><li>Near-Earth region with approximately constant g.</li><li>Air resistance neglected.</li><li>+y is declared upward for the displayed signs.</li><li>The object is in free flight after release.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Worked explanation</h4><p>A ball is thrown from a balcony with u_x = 8 m/s and u_y = 6 m/s. After release only gravity acts, and air resistance is negligible. Take +y upward and g = 10 m/s². Decide whether the ideal projectile model applies, write its component relations, and find the ball&#x27;s displacement and velocity 1 s after release.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Follow the operation, its justification and its result.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>After release the only retained interaction is gravity, so the ideal model applies: a_x = 0 and a_y = −10 m/s².</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Component relations: v_x = 8, v_y = 6 − 10t, Δx = 8t and Δy = 6t − 5t², in metres and seconds.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>At t = 1 s: Δx = 8 m and Δy = 6 − 5 = 1 m, so the displacement is (8 i + 1 j) m.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>At t = 1 s: v_x = 8 m/s and v_y = 6 − 10 = −4 m/s, so v = (8 i − 4 j) m/s.</p></div></li></ol><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>The model applies with a = (0, −10) m/s². After 1 s the displacement is (8 i + 1 j) m and the velocity is (8 i − 4 j) m/s.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>v_y is negative at 1 s, so the ball is already falling; its apex was at t = 6/10 = 0.6 s. Its speed squared, 8² + 4² = 80, equals u² − 2gΔy = 100 − 20.</p></div></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1"><h3>Decide from what acts after release whether the gravity-only model applies, and only then put a_x = 0 and a_y = −g into the component equations.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>A projectile needs a horizontal force to keep moving horizontally.</li><li>A horizontal launch has no vertical velocity throughout the flight.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If a_x=0 after release, can v_x still be nonzero?</li><li>One second after a horizontal launch, is v_y still zero under gravity?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Yes. Zero horizontal acceleration means horizontal velocity stays constant, not that it becomes zero.</li><li>No. u_y=0 only at release; a_y=-g makes v_y=-gt afterward.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Set g = 0: the equations must reduce to straight-line uniform motion with the launch velocity (u_x, u_y).</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">With +y up, v_y must fall by g every second whatever the launch direction; a v_y that grows while the object rises means the sign of g is wrong.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">2</span><h3>Name the requested event as a component condition before solving for its time: v_y = 0 at the apex, Δy = 0 at a return to launch height.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 4 of 5</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-1" aria-label="Previous section: Decide from what acts after release whether the gravity-only model applies, and only then put a_x = 0 and a_y = −g into the component equations.">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3" aria-label="Next section: Write the landing event from the actual drop while keeping the actual u_y, then read every requested quantity at that one event time.">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D3-3"><strong>Translate the requested event into the component condition that defines it before solving for time.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Apex, same-height return, ground impact and a specified later instant are different events; each is identified by a different component condition.<br><em>Result:</em> event condition chosen before calculation</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-4"><strong>For an apex event, set v_y=0 while keeping v_x=u_x and a_y=-g.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> At the highest point only the vertical velocity component is momentarily zero; gravity still accelerates downward and horizontal motion continues.<br><em>Result:</em> apex condition v_y=0 with nonzero horizontal velocity and nonzero downward acceleration</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-5"><strong>For a return to the launch height, set Delta y=0 and treat any same-height flight-time/range shortcut as conditional on that equality.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The shortcut follows from the vertical displacement returning to its initial value; it is not valid when the landing height differs.<br><em>Result:</em> same-height event condition and its validity boundary</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN" data-g9-kind="GRAPH" data-g9-min-figure-text="14" data-reveal-stages="4" data-g9-stages-total="4" data-g9-stages="VIS-KIN-2D-LEVEL-V0 VIS-KIN-2D-LEVEL-V1 VIS-KIN-2D-LEVEL-V2 VIS-KIN-2D-LEVEL-V3" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><div class="g9-diagram-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Diagram; scroll horizontally when enlarged"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 840 500" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-title">Level-ground launch with u = (6, 15) m/s: apex where v_y = 0, landing where Δy = 0</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-desc">Stage 1: a projectile launched from level ground with components u_x = 6 m/s and u_y = 15 m/s follows an arch back to the ground; two events are asked for, the highest point and the return to launch height. Stage 2: with +y up and g = 10 m/s², each event is named as a component condition: at the apex v_y = 0 while v_x stays 6 m/s and a_y stays −10 m/s², drawn as arrows at the top; at the return to launch height Δy = 0. One clock runs from t = 0 with open markers for t_apex and t_land. Stage 3: the markers are solved: 15 − 10t = 0 gives t = 1.5 s; 15t − 5t² = 0 gives t = 0 or 3 s, so the landing is at 3 s. Stage 4: the apex is 11.25 m above launch with v = (6 i + 0 j) m/s, and the range at t = 3 s is 18 m; the landing time is twice the apex time, and u_y²/(2g) = 225/20 = 11.25 m.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-LEVEL-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">Launch from level ground: two events</text>\n    <line x1="40" y1="300" x2="540" y2="300" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M150 300 Q330 -150 510 300" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>\n    <circle cx="150" cy="300" r="7" fill="currentColor"/>\n    <line x1="150" y1="300" x2="174" y2="300" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="156" y="322">u_x = 6 m/s</text>\n    <line x1="150" y1="300" x2="150" y2="240" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="142" y="238" text-anchor="end">u_y = 15 m/s</text>\n    <text x="330" y="59" text-anchor="middle">highest point (apex)</text>\n    <text x="510" y="322" text-anchor="middle">back at launch height</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-LEVEL-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="580" y="60">+y up, g = 10 m/s²</text>\n    <circle cx="330" cy="75" r="5" fill="currentColor"/>\n    <line x1="330" y1="75" x2="360" y2="75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="366" y="80">v_x = 6 m/s</text>\n    <line x1="330" y1="81" x2="330" y2="121" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="330" y="141" text-anchor="middle">a_y = −10 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="580" y="100" font-weight="600">apex:</text>\n    <text x="580" y="122">v_y = 0, v_x stays 6 m/s</text>\n    <text x="580" y="160" font-weight="600">return to launch height:</text>\n    <text x="580" y="182">Δy = 0</text>\n    <text x="40" y="404" font-weight="600">one clock for both events</text>\n    <line x1="90" y1="440" x2="486" y2="440" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2-REP-KIN-2D-LEVEL-APEX-RETURN-1--rep-kin-level-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="494" y="445">t</text>\n    <line x1="90" y1="432" x2="90" y2="448" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="90" y="466" text-anchor="middle">t = 0</text>\n    <circle cx="255" cy="440" r="7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="255" y="426" text-anchor="middle">t_apex</text>\n    <circle cx="420" cy="440" r="7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="420" y="426" text-anchor="middle">t_land</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-LEVEL-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <circle cx="255" cy="440" r="7" fill="#1f6fb2"/>\n    <circle cx="420" cy="440" r="7" fill="#1f6fb2"/>\n    <text x="255" y="466" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f6fb2">15 − 10t = 0: t = 1.5 s</text>\n    <text x="420" y="488" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1f6fb2">15t − 5t² = 0: t = 0 or 3 s</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-LEVEL-V3" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <circle cx="330" cy="75" r="7" fill="#1f6fb2"/>\n    <line x1="330" y1="151" x2="330" y2="300" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 4"/>\n    <text x="338" y="225.5" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">11.25 m</text>\n    <circle cx="510" cy="300" r="7" fill="#1f6fb2"/>\n    <path d="M150 332 V340 H510 V332" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <text x="330" y="360" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">range 18 m at t = 3 s</text>\n    <text x="580" y="222" fill="#1f6fb2">apex at 1.5 s: 11.25 m up,</text>\n    <text x="580" y="244" fill="#1f6fb2">v = (6 i + 0 j) m/s</text>\n    <text x="580" y="282">check: 3 s = 2 × 1.5 s</text>\n    <text x="580" y="304">u_y²/(2g) = 225/20 = 11.25 m</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div><div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the launch and the two events asked for.">1: Launch and events</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Name each event as a component condition on one timeline.">2: Event conditions</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Solve each condition for its time.">3: Event times</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="3" data-g9-stage-desc="Read the apex height and the range at the solved times.">4: Values at the events</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the level-ground launch with u = (6, 15) m/s and name each requested event as a component condition before its time is solved: v_y = 0 at the apex, Δy = 0 at the return to launch height.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Worked explanation</h4><p>An ideal projectile is launched from level ground with u_x = 6 m/s and u_y = 15 m/s. Take +y upward and g = 10 m/s². Name the component condition for each event, then find the time and height of the apex, and the time and range when it lands back at launch height.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Follow the operation, its justification and its result.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Apex: the condition is v_y = 0, while v_x stays 6 m/s and a_y stays −10 m/s². 15 − 10t = 0 gives t = 1.5 s.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Apex height: Δy = 15 × 1.5 − 5 × 1.5² = 22.5 − 11.25 = 11.25 m.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Landing at launch height: the condition is Δy = 0. 15t − 5t² = 0 gives t = 0 (the launch) or t = 3 s, so the landing is at t = 3 s.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Range at the same t = 3 s: Δx = 6 × 3 = 18 m.</p></div></li></ol><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>Apex at t = 1.5 s, 11.25 m above launch, with velocity (6 i + 0 j) m/s; landing at t = 3 s, 18 m from launch.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>The landing time is twice the apex time, 3 s = 2 × 1.5 s, and the apex height agrees with u_y²/(2g) = 225/20 = 11.25 m, found without any time.</p></div></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2"><h3>Name the requested event as a component condition before solving for its time: v_y = 0 at the apex, Δy = 0 at a return to launch height.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>At the highest point the whole velocity and the acceleration are both zero.</li><li>At two points of the same height on an ideal trajectory, the velocity vectors are identical.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>At the apex of an oblique projectile, which of v_x, v_y and a_y is zero?</li><li>On ascent and descent at the same height, how do the horizontal and vertical velocity components compare?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Only v_y is zero at that instant. v_x remains u_x and a_y remains -g.</li><li>v_x is the same, while v_y has equal magnitude and opposite sign; the speed matches but the velocity vector does not.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">For a return to launch height the flight time must be exactly twice the apex time, 2u_y/g against u_y/g, because the rise and the fall mirror each other.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Set u_y = 0 in the apex condition: the apex must fall at t = 0, as it does for a launch that starts level.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">3</span><h3>Write the landing event from the actual drop while keeping the actual u_y, then read every requested quantity at that one event time.</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 5 of 5</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-2" aria-label="Previous section: Name the requested event as a component condition before solving for its time: v_y = 0 at the apex, Δy = 0 at a return to launch height.">← Previous</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D3-6"><strong>For any unequal-height landing, use the actual Delta y=y_f-y_i in the vertical position equation and retain the actual u_y; for a horizontal launch specifically, set u_y=0, then obtain the event time vertically and share it with x.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Initial vertical velocity and landing displacement describe different facts. The landing event is fixed by the actual vertical geometry, while u_y=0 is only the horizontal-launch initial condition.<br><em>Result:</em> Delta y=y_f-y_i=u_y t_event-0.5g t_event^2; for horizontal launch u_y=0; reuse the solved t_event on x</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-7"><strong>At the selected event time, reconstruct the requested velocity, speed, direction, displacement or range from simultaneous components and check qualitative limits.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The reported projectile result is a consequence of one component model and one event, so its components must refer to the same instant and satisfy a_x=0, a_y=-g.<br><em>Result:</em> checked projectile consequence at one physical event</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-staged-visual" data-g9-component="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="STAGED_VISUAL" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3"><figure data-g9-figure data-g9-fig="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3-REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING" data-g9-stage="TEACHING" data-g9-representation="REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING" data-g9-kind="GRAPH" data-g9-min-figure-text="14" data-reveal-stages="4" data-g9-stages-total="4" data-g9-stages="VIS-KIN-2D-ROOF-V0 VIS-KIN-2D-ROOF-V1 VIS-KIN-2D-ROOF-V2 VIS-KIN-2D-ROOF-V3" data-g9-stage-mode="cumulative"><div class="g9-diagram-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Diagram; scroll horizontally when enlarged"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 880 520" role="img" aria-labelledby="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3-REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING-1--rep-kin-roof-title g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3-REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING-1--rep-kin-roof-desc">\n  <title id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3-REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING-1--rep-kin-roof-title">Roof launch 25 m up with u = (5, 20) m/s: landing where Δy = −25 m, keeping u_y</title>\n  <desc id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3-REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING-1--rep-kin-roof-desc">Stage 1: a ball leaves the edge of a roof 25 m above level ground with components u_x = 5 m/s and u_y = 20 m/s upward; it rises, then falls past the roof level to the ground. Stage 2: with +y up, the origin at the launch point and g = 10 m/s², the landing event is Δy = −25 m because the ground is below the launch, and the actual u_y = 20 m/s is kept; a dashed line at launch height marks the point where Δy = 0, which is not the ground. Stage 3: −25 = 20t − 5t² factorises as (t − 5)(t + 1) = 0, so t = 5 s and the root −1 s is rejected. Stage 4: at the same t = 5 s the ball lands Δx = 5 × 5 = 25 m from the building with v = (5 i − 30 j) m/s, speed √925 ≈ 30.4 m/s; without time, v² = 425 + 500 = 925 agrees.</desc>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3-REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING-1--rep-kin-roof-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>\n    <marker id="g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3-REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING-1--rep-kin-roof-arrow-acc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="#1f6fb2"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-ROOF-V0" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="20" y="26" font-weight="600">Thrown from a roof 25 m above level ground</text>\n    <rect x="100" y="250" width="70" height="200" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <line x1="40" y1="450" x2="480" y2="450" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <path d="M88 250 H80 V450 H88" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <text x="74" y="355" text-anchor="end">25 m</text>\n    <path d="M170 250 Q270 -150 370 450" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>\n    <circle cx="170" cy="250" r="7" fill="currentColor"/>\n    <line x1="170" y1="250" x2="190" y2="250" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3-REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING-1--rep-kin-roof-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="174" y="272">u_x = 5 m/s</text>\n    <line x1="170" y1="250" x2="170" y2="190" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3-REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING-1--rep-kin-roof-arrow)"/>\n    <text x="164" y="200" text-anchor="end">u_y = 20 m/s</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-ROOF-V1" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <line x1="170" y1="250" x2="470" y2="250" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 4"/>\n    <circle cx="330" cy="250" r="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>\n    <text x="342" y="272">Δy = 0 here:</text>\n    <text x="342" y="292">not the ground</text>\n    <text x="530" y="60">+y up, origin at launch, g = 10 m/s²</text>\n    <text x="530" y="100" font-weight="600">landing event:</text>\n    <text x="530" y="122">Δy = −25 m (ground below launch)</text>\n    <text x="530" y="144">keep the actual u_y = 20 m/s</text>\n    <text x="530" y="166">dotted line: launch height</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-ROOF-V2" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <text x="530" y="206" fill="#1f6fb2">−25 = 20t − 5t²</text>\n    <text x="530" y="228" fill="#1f6fb2">(t − 5)(t + 1) = 0</text>\n    <text x="530" y="250" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">t = 5 s  (t = −1 s rejected)</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-g9-stage-id="VIS-KIN-2D-ROOF-V3" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">\n    <circle cx="370" cy="450" r="7" fill="#1f6fb2"/>\n    <path d="M170 472 V480 H370 V472" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>\n    <text x="270" y="500" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600" fill="#1f6fb2">Δx = 5 × 5 = 25 m</text>\n    <line x1="400" y1="360" x2="415" y2="450" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#g9fig-CORE1A-CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3-REP-KIN-2D-ROOF-UNEQUAL-LANDING-1--rep-kin-roof-arrow-acc)"/>\n    <text x="424" y="400" fill="#1f6fb2">v = (5 i − 30 j) m/s</text>\n    <text x="530" y="290" font-weight="600">at the same t = 5 s:</text>\n    <text x="530" y="312">v_x = 5 m/s, v_y = 20 − 10 × 5 = −30 m/s</text>\n    <text x="530" y="334">speed = √925 ≈ 30.4 m/s</text>\n    <text x="530" y="370">check without t: v² = 425 + 500 = 925</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</div><div class="g9-stage-controls"><div class="g9-stage-chips" role="group" aria-label="Stages"><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="0" data-g9-stage-desc="Show the roof, the drop and the launch components.">1: The throw</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="1" data-g9-stage-desc="Write the landing event from the actual drop and keep u_y.">2: Landing condition</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="2" data-g9-stage-desc="Solve the vertical event for its physical root.">3: Landing time</button><button type="button" class="g9-stage-chip" data-g9-stage-goto="3" data-g9-stage-desc="Read distance and velocity at the same t = 5 s.">4: Values at landing</button></div><p class="g9-stage-desc" data-g9-stage-desc-text></p><div class="g9-stage-stepper"><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="prev">Previous stage</button><span data-g9-stage-label></span><button type="button" data-g9-stage-step="next">Next stage</button></div></div><figcaption data-g9-block="representation_bridge" data-g9-caption="purpose">Show the roof launch 25 m above the ground with u = (5, 20) m/s: the landing event is written from the actual drop, Δy = −25 m, with the actual u_y kept, and every requested quantity is read at that one time.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-worked-card" data-g9-component="WORKED_EXAMPLE" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="WORKED_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_anchor"><h4>Worked explanation</h4><p>A ball is thrown from the edge of a roof 25 m above level ground with u_x = 5 m/s and u_y = 20 m/s. Take +y upward and g = 10 m/s², and neglect air resistance. Find when it hits the ground, how far from the building it lands, and its impact velocity and speed.</p><p class="g9-worked-instruction">Follow the operation, its justification and its result.</p><ol class="g9-watch-steps"><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>The landing event is Δy = −25 m, because the ground is below the launch point, and u_y = 20 m/s is kept: −25 = 20t − 5t².</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Rearranged, 5t² − 20t − 25 = 0, so t² − 4t − 5 = 0 and (t − 5)(t + 1) = 0. The physical root is t = 5 s.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>Reuse t = 5 s horizontally: Δx = 5 × 5 = 25 m.</p></div></li><li data-g9-watch-step><div class="g9-worked-step-body"><p>At t = 5 s: v_x = 5 m/s and v_y = 20 − 10 × 5 = −30 m/s, so v = (5 i − 30 j) m/s and the speed is √(5² + 30²) = √925 ≈ 30.4 m/s.</p></div></li></ol><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_result"><h4>Result</h4><p>Impact at t = 5 s, 25 m from the building, with v = (5 i − 30 j) m/s and a speed of about 30.4 m/s.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="worked_check"><h4>Check</h4><p>Without time: v² = u² + 2gh = (5² + 20²) + 2 × 10 × 25 = 425 + 500 = 925 m²/s², the same speed squared as from the components.</p></div></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3"><h3>Write the landing event from the actual drop while keeping the actual u_y, then read every requested quantity at that one event time.</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>A horizontal launch has no vertical velocity throughout the flight.</li><li>Same-height flight formulas may be used whenever the question is about a projectile range or flight time.</li><li>Horizontal speed determines how long a horizontally launched object takes to fall.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>One second after a horizontal launch, is v_y still zero under gravity?</li><li>A ball launched from a roof lands on the ground below. May the same-height time-of-flight shortcut be used without checking the vertical displacement?</li><li>If the horizontal launch speed doubles from the same height, does the ideal fall time change?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. u_y=0 only at release; a_y=-g makes v_y=-gt afterward.</li><li>No. Same-height shortcuts require Delta y=0 between launch and landing. For unequal heights, solve the vertical position condition.</li><li>No. The vertical position equation determines the fall time; horizontal speed changes the range, not the fall time.</li></ul></div></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-triad" data-g9-component="QUICK_CHECK" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="TRIAD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-3"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>1-2-3 quick check</h4><ol class="g9-triad"><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">1 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">The impact speed from the components must match v² = u² + 2gh for a drop h, a route that uses no time at all.</span></li><li data-g9-triad-role=""><span class="g9-triad-head">2 · Check</span><span class="g9-triad-text">Let the drop shrink to zero: the landing time must reduce to the same-height value 2u_y/g.</span></li></ol></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A stone is launched horizontally from a cliff with speed u. With +y upward and air resistance neglected, write its component acceleration, velocity and displacement relations after release and explain why one common t is used.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>a_x=0, a_y=-g; v_x=u, v_y=-gt; Delta x=ut, Delta y=-0.5gt², all at the same elapsed time t.</p><ol><li>After release only gravity acts in the ideal model.</li><li>Gravity has no horizontal component in the chosen frame.</li><li>Both component coordinates describe the same stone at the same instant.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2018 · Paper 2 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09">JEE (Advanced) · 2019 · Paper 2 · Q9</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q5</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q6</a></li><li><a data